  "steps": [
    {
      "place": "kabul",
      "phase": "first",
      "title": "Babur in Kabul",
      "native": "ظهیرالدین محمد بابر",
      "lang": "fa",
      "when": "1504–1525",
      "at": 4,
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          71.5,
          33.0
        ],
        "width": 9
      },
      "legs": [],
      "text": "Zahir-ud-din Muhammad Babur, a Timurid prince descended from Timur and, through his mother, from Genghis Khan, lost his homeland of Fergana and failed to hold Samarkand. In 1504 he took Kabul instead. From there he raided across the Indus. In his memoir, the Baburnama, he says he had long meant to take Hindustan, which Timur had invaded in 1398.",
      "today": "Babur’s tomb stands in the Bagh-e Babur, the terraced garden in Kabul where he asked to be buried."
    },
    {
      "place": "panipat",
      "phase": "first",
      "title": "The First Battle of Panipat",
      "native": "पानीपत की पहली लड़ाई",
      "when": "21 April 1526",
      "at": 14,
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          73.0,
          32.0
        ],
        "width": 12
      },
      "legs": [
        {
          "from": "kabul",
          "to": "panipat",
          "traveller": "babur-army",
          "style": "mughal",
          "bend": -0.1
        }
      ],
      "text": "Babur marched from Kabul in late 1525 and met Ibrahim Lodi, the Delhi sultan, at Panipat. Lodi’s army was far larger, though the numbers are disputed. Babur chained carts together behind a ditch, with field guns and matchlocks between them, and sent horsemen round both flanks. Ibrahim Lodi was killed with thousands of his men, and the Delhi Sultanate ended.",
      "today": "Ibrahim Lodi’s tomb, a plain platform restored by the British in 1866, stands in Panipat."
    },
    {
      "place": "agra",
      "phase": "first",
      "title": "Agra and Khanwa",
      "when": "1526–1530",
      "at": 24,
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          77.6,
          28.4
        ],
        "width": 8
      },
      "legs": [],
      "text": "Babur sent his son Humayun to secure the treasury at Agra and made it his capital. In March 1527 he faced a greater threat: a confederacy of Rajput rulers led by Rana Sanga of Mewar. At Khanwa, west of Agra, the same carts and guns won again. Babur died in 1530, and Humayun inherited a young and unsettled empire.",
      "today": "Khanwa is a village in Bharatpur district, Rajasthan; Babur’s first garden in Agra, the Ram Bagh, survives by the Yamuna."
    },
    {
      "place": "kalanaur",
      "phase": "second",
      "title": "Akbar crowned at Kalanaur",
      "native": "अकबर",
      "when": "1540–1556",
      "at": 36,
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          76.0,
          31.2
        ],
        "width": 8
      },
      "legs": [],
      "text": "Sher Shah Suri drove Humayun out of India in 1540, and Humayun spent fifteen years in exile. He retook Delhi in 1555 but died months later after falling on the stairs of his library. His son Akbar, aged thirteen, was crowned in a garden at Kalanaur in Punjab on 14 February 1556, with Bairam Khan as regent.",
      "today": "The brick throne platform of Akbar’s coronation, the Takht-i-Akbari, still stands outside Kalanaur."
    },
    {
      "place": "delhi",
      "phase": "second",
      "title": "Hemu takes Delhi",
      "native": "हेमचंद्र विक्रमादित्य",
      "when": "October 1556",
      "at": 44,
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          77.2,
          29.2
        ],
        "width": 8
      },
      "legs": [],
      "text": "Hemu, a Hindu of modest birth, rose from supplying the army to become chief minister and general of Adil Shah Sur, and is said to have won many battles. After Humayun’s death he took Agra, and on 7 October 1556 beat the Mughal governor at Tughlaqabad outside Delhi. He took the city and the title Vikramaditya.",
      "today": "The ruins of Tughlaqabad, a fort city of the Delhi sultans, stand on the southern edge of Delhi."
    },
    {
      "place": "panipat",
      "phase": "second",
      "title": "The Second Battle of Panipat",
      "native": "पानीपत की दूसरी लड़ाई",
      "when": "5 November 1556",
      "at": 50,
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          76.2,
          30.6
        ],
        "width": 8
      },
      "legs": [
        {
          "from": "kalanaur",
          "to": "panipat",
          "traveller": "mughal-army",
          "style": "mughal",
          "bend": 0.1
        }
      ],
      "text": "The Mughal army under Bairam Khan met Hemu at Panipat. Days earlier a Mughal detachment had captured Hemu’s artillery. Hemu, leading from an elephant, was close to winning when an arrow struck his eye and he fell unconscious. His army broke. He was taken to the young Akbar and beheaded; accounts disagree over whose hand struck the blow.",
      "today": "Hemu’s memorial (samadhi) at Shodapur, on the edge of Panipat, marks where tradition says he was beheaded."
    },
    {
      "place": "attock",
      "phase": "third",
      "title": "The Marathas at Attock",
      "native": "अटकेपार",
      "lang": "mr",
      "when": "1757–1758",
      "at": 60,
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          73.5,
          32.5
        ],
        "width": 11
      },
      "legs": [],
      "text": "By the 1750s the Mughal emperors were weak, and the Marathas, governed from Pune by the Peshwas, collected tribute across much of India. In 1757 Ahmad Shah Durrani of Afghanistan sacked Delhi and Mathura. The next year a Maratha army under Raghunathrao drove the Afghans out of Punjab, took Lahore and reached Attock on the Indus.",
      "today": "Attock Fort, built by Akbar in 1583, still guards the Indus crossing in Pakistan; Marathi keeps the phrase “beyond Attock” for a far-reaching triumph."
    },
    {
      "place": "barari-ghat",
      "phase": "third",
      "title": "Barari Ghat",
      "when": "January 1760",
      "at": 66,
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          73.5,
          31.0
        ],
        "width": 14
      },
      "legs": [
        {
          "from": "kandahar",
          "to": "barari-ghat",
          "traveller": "durrani-army",
          "style": "afghan",
          "bend": 0.1
        }
      ],
      "text": "Ahmad Shah Durrani came back in late 1759 to retake Punjab and to help the Rohilla chief Najib ud-Daula, who had been fighting the Marathas. At Barari Ghat, a ford on the Yamuna just north of Delhi, the Afghans attacked a Maratha force in January 1760 and killed its commander, Dattaji Shinde. Durrani then wintered in the Ganga–Yamuna Doab.",
      "today": "Burari is now a crowded neighbourhood of north Delhi beside the Yamuna."
    },
    {
      "place": "pune",
      "phase": "third",
      "title": "Bhau marches north",
      "native": "सदाशिवराव भाऊ",
      "lang": "mr",
      "when": "March–October 1760",
      "at": 72,
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          75.5,
          23.5
        ],
        "width": 16
      },
      "legs": [
        {
          "from": "pune",
          "to": "delhi",
          "traveller": "maratha-army",
          "style": "maratha",
          "bend": -0.1
        }
      ],
      "text": "Peshwa Balaji Baji Rao sent a great army north under his cousin Sadashivrao Bhau, with his own son and heir, Vishwasrao. It brought Ibrahim Khan Gardi’s artillery and a crowd of pilgrims and camp followers. Bhau took Delhi in August 1760, and in October stormed the Afghan post at Kunjpura, north of Panipat. Durrani crossed the Yamuna behind him.",
      "today": "Shaniwar Wada, the Peshwas’ palace in Pune, burned in 1828; its walls and gates still stand."
    },
    {
      "place": "panipat",
      "phase": "third",
      "title": "The siege at Panipat",
      "when": "November 1760 – January 1761",
      "at": 78,
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          77.0,
          29.0
        ],
        "width": 8
      },
      "legs": [],
      "text": "Cut off from Delhi and the south, Bhau dug in round Panipat, and Durrani camped facing him. For two months each side raided the other’s supplies. Afghan patrols killed the Maratha supply officer Govind Pant Bundele, and food ran out in the crowded Maratha camp. By January men and horses were starving, and Bhau chose to fight his way out.",
      "today": "Panipat sits on the old Grand Trunk Road, the route every army from the north-west took to Delhi."
    },
    {
      "place": "panipat",
      "phase": "third",
      "title": "The Third Battle of Panipat",
      "native": "पानिपतची तिसरी लढाई",
      "lang": "mr",
      "when": "14 January 1761",
      "at": 84,
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          77.0,
          29.1
        ],
        "width": 8
      },
      "legs": [],
      "text": "At dawn the Marathas attacked. Ibrahim Khan Gardi’s guns and infantry broke the Rohilla wing, but by afternoon Durrani’s reserves and swivel guns mounted on camels (zamburak) turned the battle. Vishwasrao was shot, and Bhau rode into the fight and was killed. The army collapsed, and captives were massacred next day. Deaths are disputed estimates, in the tens of thousands.",
      "today": "In Marathi, “a Panipat” still means a crushing disaster."
    },
    {
      "place": "pune",
      "phase": "third",
      "title": "After Panipat",
      "when": "1761–1772",
      "at": 92,
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          74.5,
          20.5
        ],
        "width": 11
      },
      "legs": [],
      "text": "Peshwa Balaji Baji Rao, already ill, died in Pune in June 1761, tradition says of grief. Durrani, with unpaid troops and Sikh risings in Punjab, went home. Within ten years the Marathas were back: Mahadji Shinde, lamed at Panipat, escorted the emperor Shah Alam II to Delhi in 1772. Many see the British as the real gainers.",
      "today": "Pune is a city of some seven million people, a centre of Maratha history and of India’s software industry."
    },
    {
      "place": "panipat",
      "phase": "memory",
      "title": "Panipat remembered",
      "when": "Today",
      "at": 98,
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          77.0,
          29.2
        ],
        "width": 8
      },
      "legs": [],
      "text": "Panipat keeps all three battles. Ibrahim Lodi’s tomb stands in the town, and Hemu’s memorial marks where, tradition says, he died. A black mango tree (Kala Amb) once marked where the fighting of 1761 was fiercest; a memorial stands there now, and a war memorial was added in Panipat in 2012. The Panipat Museum tells the story of all three.",
      "today": "Panipat, in Haryana, about 90 kilometres north of Delhi, is now known for its textile mills."
    }
  ],
